gene regulation can occur at many  different levels so it can go right at  the beginning in the activation of the  gene in the DNA in the genome through  the synthesis of the RNA through its  splicing through export from the nucleus  through translation in the cytoplasm  through degradation of the RNA all of  these steps can be regulated and in fact  most of them are under different  circumstances gene regulation is the way  that different genes are turned on and  off in different cells to give us  different cells different organs and  ultimately different organisms so the  way we used to think about RNA was as  the middleman between DNA and protein  you've got DNA which encodes the genes  carries all the information that gets  passed down from generation to  generation and proteins of course are  what makes up most of our cells and body  the hair cell the hair the skin cells  bone etcetera etcetera is made up of  proteins and so RNA had this reputation  as being the the in-between of those two  things and in fact what we know now is  that RNA was probably the original  molecule of life this idea that the  original replicating chemical entity  might have actually been made of RNA RNA  can do base pairing the same way DNA can  and so you can get replication but RNA  also can fold up and into interesting  three-dimensional molecules the same way  that proteins can and so it can act as  an enzyme to catalyze chemical reactions  that are necessary for a life question  then is why do we have DNA in the  nucleus and the answer under that  scenario would be that that DNA is a  good storage molecule is much more  stable in RNA and then in fact it you  served RNA at some point in evolution as  the information molecule and then left  RNA in at least until until a few years  ago in this poultry informational role  but now we're beginning to appreciate  and in fact RNA might be much more  central to the metabolism of the cell  and regulation of the cell than we  previously thought our view of the  genome is is changing and changing very  quickly it for a long time it was seen  as a kind of desert of evolutionary junk  with the occasional island of protein  coding sequences but now our view is  changing and not everybody agrees with  this and not everybody is even aware of  this change but it is changing quickly  to a view of that the genome is really  an extremely sophisticated information  suite and that most of the information  being transacted by the genome is being  transacted through RNA regulatory  networks which set the settings for the  control of growth and development in  multicellular organisms the story is how  you integrate all of this new  information how proteins work and rnase  work in the context of each other  clearly transcriptional regulatory  systems must be integrated and so I  think the critical question is how if  you think about a human we have roughly  a hundred trillion cells in a very very  precise arrangement you know all of the  dozens of muscles in your face to create  the range of human expressions the bones  and the finger and the brain and the  information for programming the ontogeny  and functioning of this has to lie  somewhere we have the same number of  protein coding genes many of which are  equivalent between us and as a simple  nematode worm that only has 1,000 cells  so go figure  you had information in DNA that  information got transferred to an RNA  intermediate and that RNA information  got translated into protein and of  course that's still true but now we know  that the information is flowing in much  more interesting ways and that there's  information flowing in both directions  the information can flow also from the  RNA back to the to the DNA and so I  think we have to think of the the genome  as not just a central information  repository but something that's  interacting constantly with its  environment and many of those  interactions are going to be carried out  by RNA and it looks very much like  evolution discovered through the use of  RNA the power of digital communication  and control systems are billion years  we did now micro RNA is small RNA  molecules are roughly 22 nucleotides in  length they have no catalytic activity  whatsoever as far as we know what they  do is recognize a target sequence  another RNA usually a messenger RNA and  a target that messenger RNA thereby for  to inhibit this translation and perhaps  to its destruction and that is mediated  by a generic protein complex called a  RISC complex so you can dial up  regulatory circuits but simply by  producing a different micro RNA you  don't have to reproduce an entire  infrastructure the infrastructure is  generic so this is what I mean by a  digital control system I don't mean in  this context on off but rather a very  compact sequence specific string that  conveys information to a target which  then recruits the appropriate  infrastructure to convert that message  back into an analogue action now if you  think about it exactly the same thing  happens when you order a DVD online you  send digital signal so the product code  in your credit card number which whizzed  over the wires or optical fibers and  then at the end of the line somebody  interprets that message and puts a DVD  in a bag and post it to so the  information transfer is far more  efficient far more flexible and and far  more malleable in an evolutionary  context I think we'll see in the future  an increasing role for RNA in gene  regulation I think we'll see expansion  of the systems we know about more  excitingly we're going to discover new  systems that I'm sure of that are RNA  based we're just realizing that we've  only got to first base and we have a  long way to go and most of the journey  forward is going to be dissecting  analyzing and and rebuilding an  understanding of these massively  parallel and extremely sophisticated RNA  regulatory circuits which really do  underpin our complexity and the irony I  think is that what was dismissed as junk  because it wasn't understood will turn  out to hold the secret of human  complexity including our cognitive  complex  and that's where we're going over the  next 10 to 50 years  you  science is published by triple-a s the  science Society
 

